    "q-80757": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80757",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{y}+3x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+xy+5$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y)=3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{y}+3x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+xy+5)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
